{
  "result": "At dawn, the city belongs briefly to the people who clean it. Street sweepers guide their brooms along the curb, bakers lift warm loaves from ovens, and buses sigh awake beneath pale windows. The day has not yet chosen its mood. A shopkeeper unlocks a blue door. A cyclist coasts through an empty intersection, carrying nothing urgent but the pleasure of moving.\n\nThere is a quiet generosity in these early hours. No one asks the sky to be spectacular; it is enough that it brightens. No one applauds the baker, though the bread will gather strangers around a table. Work begins without ceremony, and the world becomes usable one small act at a time.\n\nI like to imagine that every morning is a letter addressed to nobody in particular. Its message is simple: begin again, but not necessarily brilliantly. Begin with the kettle, the open window, the apology you have been postponing. Begin by noticing the neighbor\u2019s lamp still on, or the tree pushing a new leaf through last week\u2019s rain.\n\nLater, the streets will fill with horns and errands, and the day will acquire its familiar weight. Yet somewhere beneath the noise, that first gentleness remains. It is in the bread torn and shared, the curb made clear, the door held open for a stranger. Perhaps hope is less a grand promise than a practiced habit: the daily decision to make room for what might arrive. Ordinary hands, moving carefully, can leave the morning kinder than they found it.",
